%[text] %[text:anchor:H_16353E39] ## Electric vehicles: the wave of the future?
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![EVDrawingV11.png](text:image:60ee)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Essential components of an electric vehicle*
%[text] Electric vehicles (EV) may well be the future of environmentally sustainable travel. But what makes it possible for an EV to be more efficient than a gas vehicle while still providing enough power to accelerate to and maintain high speeds? The answer lies in efficient power electronics. In this module, you will learn about transfer functions and use them to analyze the dynamics of several essential components of an electric vehicle.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_0742657D] # Transfer Function Basics
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7A06E214] ![1601576787454.png](text:image:11a9) This live script is intended to be used with the code shown. Select the `VIEW` tab and switch to `Output Inline`.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D61F7C12] ## Transfer Function Definition
%[text] Consider a dynamic system with a single input function $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"} and a single output function$y(t)${"altText":"y(t)"}:
%[text]  ![SystemBoxColor.png](text:image:01fc)
%[text] This is known as a **SISO** (Single Input Single Output) system. In general, the system $g${"altText":"g"} is defined through a differential equation: $g(u, x, \\dot{x}, \\ddot{x},  \\ldots) = 0${"altText":"g(u, x, \dot{x}, \ddot{x},  \ldots) = 0"}.
%[text] The **transfer function** is defined as
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{Y(s)}{U(s)}"},
%[text] the ratio of the Laplace transform of the output $Y(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{y(t)\\}${"altText":"Y(s) = \mathcal{L}{y(t)}"} to the Laplace transform of the input $U(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}${"altText":"U(s) = \mathcal{L}{u(t)}"}. The transfer function $G(s)${"altText":"G(s)"} maps inputs $U${"altText":"U"} to outputs $Y${"altText":"Y"}:
%[text]  ![SystemBoxSDomainColor.png](text:image:7005)
%[text] ![brightbulb.png](text:image:8afc)  If you need to brush up on Laplace transforms, review them in: [ConceptReview.m](file:./ConceptReview.m). Note that this link will only work if ConceptReview.m is in the current folder or the MATLAB Search Path.
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:7386) **Example.** Find the transfer function of a mass-spring-damper that is dynamically forced by an arbitrary function $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}. Assume that the system is initially at rest ($x(0) = 0${"altText":"x(0) = 0"} and $\\dot{x}(0)=0${"altText":"\dot{x}(0)=0"}).
%[text]  ![mass\_spring\_damper2-flipped.png](text:image:1844)
%[text]  *Mass-spring-damper diagram*
%[text] **Solution.** The equation of motion for a mass-spring-damper is
%[text]  $m \\ddot{x} + c \\dot{x} + k x = u${"altText":"m \ddot{x} + c \dot{x} + k x = u"}
%[text] Using the properties of the Laplace transform for derivatives, the Laplace transform is
%[text]  $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{ m \\ddot{x} + c\\dot{x} + kx = u \\right\\}${"altText":"\mathcal{L} \left{ m \ddot{x} + c\dot{x} + kx = u \right}"} $\\rightarrow${"altText":"\rightarrow"} $m\[s^2 X - sx(0) - \\dot{x}(0)\] + c \[s X - x(0)\] + kX = U${"altText":"m\[s^2 X - sx(0) - \dot{x}(0)\] + c \[s X - x(0)\] + kX = U"}
%[text] Applying $x(0) = 0${"altText":"x(0) = 0"} and $\\dot{x}(0)=0${"altText":"\dot{x}(0)=0"}:
%[text]  $ms^2 X  + c s X  + kX = U${"altText":"ms^2 X  + c s X  + kX = U"}
%[text] Solving for the ratio of the input to the output yields the transfer function:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{m s^2 + cs + k}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{m s^2 + cs + k}"}
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:5998) **Exercise.** Find the transfer function of a simple pendulum with zero initial conditions using the linearized equations of motion. Consider that the pendulum is being driven by an arbitrary forcing function $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"} (note that any angle dependence of the forcing would be baked into $u${"altText":"u"}). The equations of motion are: 
%[text]  $\\ddot{\\theta} + \\frac{g}{l} \\theta = u${"altText":"\ddot{\theta} + \frac{g}{l} \theta = u"}
%[text] where $g${"altText":"g"} is acceleration due to gravity and $l${"altText":"l"} is the length of the pendulum. Record your answer below in variable `pendulumTF` in terms of the symbolic variables `g`, `l`, and `s`.
syms g l s 
% Record your answer in pendulumTF
pendulumTF = NaN;
checkPendulumTF(pendulumTF) % Checks your answer
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_61EAA13A] ## Electric vehicle dynamic system
%[text] In this module, you'll investigate the dynamics of components of an electric vehicle's electrical and mechanical systems (EV) using transfer functions. The major components of the EV system are represented in the diagram below.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![EVSimpleSystem2.png](text:image:5b2b)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Simplified EV power system*
%[text] There are two domains represented in this diagram: the electrical and physical domains. The DC motor couples the domains by converting electrical energy to mechanical. Inside the electrical domain, there are two essential conversions.
%[text] 1. The AC power source (120/240V) is converted to a high voltage DC source (200-800V) used to charge the battery pack.
%[text] 2. The battery's high voltage DC (200-800V) is converted to a low voltage (12V or 48V) by a buck converter for use by low voltage systems. \
%[text] In this module, you will investigate several paradigmatic components present (or like those present) in EVs. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B794AC9C] ## Transfer function representation of simple vehicle motion
%[text] The most intuitive part of the electric vehicle system is the motion of the vehicle itself.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![EVSimpleSystem-motionFocus.png](text:image:4cfe)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *In this live script, you'll focus on modeling the motion of the vehicle.*
%[text] In a straightforward model, an EV (and indeed, any vehicle) can be modeled as a simple mass with two forces acting on it: a traction force and a frictional force. The traction force results from the motor applying torque to the wheels.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![simpleCar.png](text:image:306b)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *A simple vehicle model*
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:7ffc) **Example. (a)** Derive the equation of motion for the simple vehicle model and **(b)** compute the transfer function of the displacement $x(t)${"altText":"x(t)"} using the traction force as the input function $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}.
%[text] **Solution.** 
%[text] **(a)** The displacement of the vehicle is $x(t)${"altText":"x(t)"}. For simplicity, the frictional force is assumed to be proportional to the velocity $\\dot{x}${"altText":"\dot{x}"} with a constant of proportionality $k${"altText":"k"}:
%[text]  $F\_{friction} = k \\dot{x}${"altText":"F\_{friction} = k \dot{x}"}
%[text] The traction force varies depending on the output of the motor (among other considerations), so it is treated as an arbitrary function:
%[text]  $F\_{traction} = u(t)${"altText":"F\_{traction} = u(t)"}
%[text] With these two forces, Newton's second law implies:
%[text]  $m\\ddot{x} = -k\\dot{x}  + u(t)${"altText":"m\ddot{x} = -k\dot{x}  + u(t)"}
%[text] **(b)** Computing the Laplace transform of the equation of motion yields
%[text]  $m\[s^2 X - sx(0) - x'(0)\] = - k \[s X - x(0)\] + U${"altText":"m\[s^2 X - sx(0) - x'(0)\] = - k \[s X - x(0)\] + U"}
%[text] Applying the zero initial conditions and solving for $X/U${"altText":"X\/U"} yields
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}"}
%[text] ![1601576787454.png](text:image:1dea) **Note.** There are many simplifications present in this vehicle model (especially in the way the frictional forces are modeled). The model is intended to help illustrate the essential concepts of transfer functions, rather than accurately account for the forces present in the true mechanical system.
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:2351) **Exercise.** It may be the case that you want to analyze the velocity $v(t)${"altText":"v(t)"} rather than the position $x${"altText":"x"}. Rewrite the equation of motion 
%[text]  $m\\ddot{x} = -k\\dot{x}  + f(t)${"altText":"m\ddot{x} = -k\dot{x}  + f(t)"}
%[text] in terms of the velocity, $v(t) = \\dot{x}(t)${"altText":"v(t) = \dot{x}(t)"}, and then solve for the transfer function of $v${"altText":"v"}: $H(s) = V(s)/F(s)${"altText":"H(s) = V(s)\/F(s)"}. Record your answer below in `Vtf` in terms of the symbolic variables `s`, `m`, and `k`, and then run the section to check your result.
syms m s k % Symbolic variable declarations
% Record your answer here
Vtf = NaN;

checkVTF(Vtf) % Checks your answer
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3E8817B9] ## Impulse response 
%[text] The impulse response of a system is the output generated from an impulse input. This is also commonly referred to as the natural response.
%[text]  ![SystemBoxImpulse.png](text:image:7b89)
%[text]  *Response of a system to an impulse. While the response may be computed in the* $s${"altText":"s"}*-domain, it is shown in the time domain.*
%[text] Here $\\delta(t)${"altText":"\delta(t)"} is the Dirac delta function which has the properties:
%[text]  $\\int\_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\delta(t) dt = 1 ${"altText":"\int\_{-\infty}^{\infty} \delta(t) dt = 1 "}   and   $\\delta(t) = 0 \\text{ for } t \\neq 0${"altText":"\delta(t) = 0 \text{ for } t \neq 0"}
%[text] Think of the unit impulse as giving the system a (very) quick shove at time $t=0${"altText":"t=0"}. The impulse response shows how the system will respond to that shove.
%[text] ![brightbulb.png](text:image:3aed) **Reflect.** Consider the simple vehicle motion model. The input function is the traction force $u(t) = F\_{traction}(t)${"altText":"u(t) = F\_{traction}(t)"}. An impulse input corresponds to an instantaneous force at time $t=0${"altText":"t=0"}.
%[text]  ![simpleCar.png](text:image:707a)
%[text] - Try drawing a plot of the impulse response of the position transfer function $X(s)/F(s)${"altText":"X(s)\/F(s)"} in the time domain.
%[text] - Try drawing a plot of the impulse response of the velocity transfer function $V(s)/F(s)${"altText":"V(s)\/F(s)"} in the time domain. \
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:61ad) **Example.** Plot the impulse response of a mass-spring-damper with zero initial conditions and parameters $m = 1${"altText":"m = 1"}, $c = 0.1${"altText":"c = 0.1"}, and $k = 0.5${"altText":"k = 0.5"}. 
%[text]  ![mass\_spring\_damper2-flipped.png](text:image:50ad)
%[text]  *Mass-spring-damper diagram*
%[text] Recall the transfer function of the mass-spring-damper:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{m s^2 + cs + k}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{m s^2 + cs + k}"}
%[text] **Solution.**
%[text] You can compute the impulse response in MATLAB by defining a transfer function using [`tf`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/control/ref/tf.html) and then evaluating its response using the [`impulse`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/control/ref/dynamicsystem.impulse.html) function.
%[text] The `tf` function has the syntax
%[text]  `tf(num,denom)`
%[text] where `num` represents the coefficients of the numerator and `denom` represents those of the denominator in descending order. Note that a transfer function created using the `tf` function is not a symbolic function and cannot be manipulated with the Symbolic Math Toolbox.
%[text] The numerator and denominator of $G${"altText":"G"} are defined below.
m = 1; % Default: 1 %[control:slider:5631]{"position":[5,6]}
c = 0.1; % Default: 0.1 %[control:slider:23f2]{"position":[5,8]}
k = 0.4; % Default: 0.4 %[control:slider:3e12]{"position":[5,8]}
num = [0 0 1];
denom = [m c k];
%[text] Call `tf` to build the transfer function.
G = tf(num,denom)
%[text] To plot the impulse response, call the `impulse` function with the syntax:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] impulse(H,tFinal)
%[text] ```
%[text] where `H` is the transfer function and `tFinal` is the duration of the simulation.
impulse(G,100)
ylabel("x [m]")
%[text] ![brightbulb.png](text:image:82c2) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - How are the damping $c${"altText":"c"} and spring stiffness $k${"altText":"k"} reflected in the impulse response?
%[text] - If $c${"altText":"c"} is increased to 0.5, how will the response change?
%[text] - Try setting $c = 0.5${"altText":"c = 0.5"} and recompute the impulse response.
%[text] - Try setting $k = 1${"altText":"k = 1"} and recompute the impulse response. \
%%
