%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B2078600] ## Analytic computation of the impulse and step responses
%[text] At this point, you may have wondered: how are the impulse and step responses calculated? In MATLAB, they are numerically estimated. In many common cases, it is also possible to derive the response analytically. While in practice solving for the analytic response is typically unnecessary, it is a valuable exercise to develop further intuition.
%[text] To analytically solve for the impulse or step response:
%[text] 1. take the Laplace transform of the input function
%[text] 2. solve for $X(s)${"altText":"X(s)"}
%[text] 3. back-transform $X(s)${"altText":"X(s)"} to obtain the time-domain response $x(t)${"altText":"x(t)"} \
%[text] If you don't want to perform Step 1 by hand, you can simply use a table or the `laplace` function. You can complete Step 3 using a transform table or the [`ilaplace`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.ilaplace.html) function from the Symbolic Math Toolbox.
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:0f45) **Example.** 
%[text] **(a)** Compute the analytic impulse response for the simple vehicle model using its position transfer function.
%[text] **(b)** Set $m = 1300${"altText":"m = 1300"} and $k = 100${"altText":"k = 100"}. Then plot the analytic impulse result and compare it to the solution found using the `impulse` function.
%[text] **Solution.** 
%[text] **(a)** Recall that the position transfer function for the simple vehicle model was:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}"}
%[text] Start by computing the Laplace transform of the input forcing function $\\delta(t)${"altText":"\delta(t)"}:
%[text]  $U(s) = \\int\_{0}^\\infty \\delta(t) e^{-st} dt = e^{-st}|\_{t = 0} = 1${"altText":"U(s) = \int\_{0}^\infty \delta(t) e^{-st} dt = e^{-st}|\_{t = 0} = 1"}
%[text] This implies that
%[text]  $X(s)= \\frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}${"altText":"X(s)= \frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}"}
%[text] At this point, you can refer to a table or use the `ilaplace` function to evaluate the inverse Laplace transform.
syms m k positive
syms s x
X = 1/(m*s^2 + k*s)
x = ilaplace(X)
%%
%[text] **(b)** First define the transfer function using `tf` and then compute the impulse response.
m = 1300;
k = 100;
Xs = tf([0 0 1],[m k 0]);
[x,t] = impulse(Xs);
%[text] Now compute the analytic solution on the same interval and plot the comparison.
xAnalytic = 1/k - exp(-k*t/m)/k;
plot(t,x,t,xAnalytic,'--')
legend("result of impulse", "analytic")
xlabel("t [s]")
ylabel("x [m]")
title("Step response")
%%
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:2129) **Exercise.** 
%[text] **(a)** Compute the analytic **step** response for the vehicle position transfer function. Recall that the position transfer function for this simple vehicle model was:
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{ms^2 + ks}"}
%[text] Store the output in symbolic variable x using the pre-defined symbolic variables.
% Use these symbolic variables
syms m k real
syms s x 

% Compute the symbolic form of the step response and store it in x
x = NaN
checkAnalyticStep(x)
%%
%[text] **(b)** Set $m = 1300${"altText":"m = 1300"} and $k = 100${"altText":"k = 100"}. Then plot the analytic step result you computed in **(a)**. Also, plot the step response calculated using the `step` function.
% This computes the step response 
m = 1300;
k = 100;
Xs = tf([0 0 1],[m k 0]);
[x,t] = step(Xs,20);
