%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D44F7E73] ## Forced response
%[text] It is also possible to compute the response of a system to an arbitrary input function $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}. 
%[text]  ![SystemBoxForced.png](text:image:3dc2)
%[text]  *Response of a system to a square wave.*
%[text] Analytically, this can be accomplished by computing $U(s)${"altText":"U(s)"} and subsequently finding the inverse Laplace transform of $X(s)${"altText":"X(s)"}. In cases where the analytic expression does not exist, you can estimate the response numerically using the [`lsim`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/control/ref/dynamicsystem.lsim.html) function.
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:0337) **Activity.**
%[text]     In this activity, you will analyze the response of the mass-spring-damper system
%[text]  $G(s) = \\frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{1}{m s^2 + cs + k}${"altText":"G(s) = \frac{X(s)}{U(s)} = \frac{1}{m s^2 + cs + k}"}
%[text]     The mass-spring-damper is assumed to start at rest and is driven by an input function $u(t)${"altText":"u(t)"}.
%[text] **Part 1.** Familiarize yourself with the activity.
%[text] 1. Adjust the input function `u` using the dropdown and observe the responses.
%[text] 2. Adjust the mass-spring-damper parameters and the input frequency to observe the changes to the response.
%[text] 3. Check the "animate" box to show an animation of the mass-spring-damper. You can turn this on or off during the remainder of the activity as you prefer. \
%[text] **Part 2.** Find the resonant frequency.
%[text] 1. It is a well-known phenomenon that systems will become unstable if they are driven at their resonant frequency. Try extending the simulation time by increasing `tfinal`. Is the system being driven at the resonant frequency?
%[text] 2. You should notice that the transient response is characterized by two frequencies: the driving frequency and the mass-spring-damper's natural frequency. Is the natural frequency of the oscillator larger or smaller than the driving (input) frequency?
%[text] 3. Adjust the input frequency so that it matches the natural frequency of the mass-spring-damper. Do you observe resonant oscillations?
%[text] 4. Set the damping to zero. If you are at (or very close to) the resonant frequency, you should observe that the response will grow without bound.
%[text] 5. Try driving the oscillator with a different input function. Does the function choice affect the resonant oscillations? \
m = 1; % Default: 1 %[control:slider:28d3]{"position":[5,6]}
c = 0.1; % Default: 0.1 %[control:slider:4b78]{"position":[5,8]}
k = 1; % Default: 1 %[control:slider:0573]{"position":[5,6]}
freq = 0.25; % Frequency in Hz. Default: 0.25 %[control:slider:09c3]{"position":[8,12]}
tfinal = 30; % Simulation duration. Default: 30 %[control:slider:20bc]{"position":[10,12]}
t = linspace(0,tfinal,500);
u = sin(2*pi*freq*t).^2; %[control:dropdown:73dd]{"position":[5,24]}
animate = false; %[control:checkbox:9847]{"position":[11,16]}
  %[control:button:1e91]{"position":[1,2]}

% Compute and plot the response
G = tf([0 0 1],[m c k]); % The transfer function
[x,t] = lsim(G,u,t); % Simulate the response using lsim
if(~animate)
    plotResponse(t,u,x) % regular plot
else 
    animateSingleMSD(t,x,u,0.5,200) % animated response
end
